2.34.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:

```rust
MmWithUser::into_mmput_async(self.mm.mmget_not_zero().ok_or(ESRCH)?)
    .mmap_read_lock()
    .vma_lookup(vma_addr)
    .ok_or(ESRCH)?
    .as_mixedmap_vma()
    .ok_or(ESRCH)?
    .vm_insert_page(user_page_addr, &new_page)
```

```rust
if let Some(vma) = mmap_read.vma_lookup(vma_addr) {
    let user_page_addr = vma_addr + (page_index << PAGE_SHIFT);
    vma.zap_page_range_single(user_page_addr, PAGE_SIZE);
}
```

Reason:  
These code snippets perform VMA lookup only by address (`vma_lookup`), then immediately call `vm_insert_page` or `zap_page_range_single` on the returned VMA without verifying that it is the same VMA originally registered by Rust Binder. If another thread closes the original VMA and maps a new one at the same address, these operations apply to the wrong VMA, potentially allowing writes to read-only binder pages or corrupting other mappings.

The code statements that fix the bug are:

```rust
static BINDER_VM_OPS: bindings::vm_operations_struct = pin_init::zeroed();

fn check_vma(vma: &virt::VmaRef, owner: *const ShrinkablePageRange) -> Option<&virt::VmaMixedMap> {
    let vm_ops = unsafe { (*vma.as_ptr()).vm_ops };
    if !ptr::eq(vm_ops, &BINDER_VM_OPS) {
        return None;
    }
    let vm_private_data = unsafe { (*vma.as_ptr()).vm_private_data };
    if !ptr::eq(vm_private_data, owner.cast()) {
        return None;
    }
    vma.as_mixedmap_vma()
}
```

```rust
unsafe {
    (*vma.as_ptr()).vm_private_data = ptr::from_ref(self).cast_mut().cast::<c_void>()
};

unsafe { (*vma.as_ptr()).vm_ops = &BINDER_VM_OPS };
```

```rust
check_vma(
    MmWithUser::into_mmput_async(self.mm.mmget_not_zero().ok_or(ESRCH)?)
        .mmap_read_lock()
        .vma_lookup(vma_addr)
        .ok_or(ESRCH)?,
    self,
)
.ok_or(ESRCH)?
.vm_insert_page(user_page_addr, &new_page)
```

```rust
if let Some(unchecked_vma) = mmap_read.vma_lookup(vma_addr) {
    if let Some(vma) = check_vma(unchecked_vma, range_ptr) {
        let user_page_addr = vma_addr + (page_index << PAGE_SHIFT);
        vma.zap_page_range_single(user_page_addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    }
}
```

Reason:  
The fix introduces `check_vma`, which verifies that the VMA found by `vma_lookup` has the expected `vm_ops` and `vm_private_data` (set during VMA registration). By storing a unique ops struct and a pointer to the owning object in the VMA, the function ensures that the VMA belongs to Rust Binder before allowing page insertion or zapping. The original direct calls are replaced with calls that first pass the VMA through `check_vma`, thus preventing operations on foreign VMAs.

